./target/specs/hal-engine-spec-anchor-drift/spec.md:8:A citation written `([validated by](../../src/X.test.ts#Lnn))` names a line, and a line number is the least stable thing a file has. Insert eight lines above it and the anchor still resolves, still renders, and now points at something else entirely — the spec reads as cited while citing the wrong thing, and nothing about it looks wrong. `scripts/repoint-spec-anchors.mjs` closes that: it resolves each anchor to the content it cited in a baseline ref, finds that content in the working copy, and rewrites the number. `--check` is the CI gate that refuses drift; a plain run is the fix.
./target/specs/hal-engine-spec-anchor-drift/spec.md:12:Any relative path with a file extension followed by `#L<n>`, in any spec under `specs/<slug>/spec.md` plus `.specify/spec.md`. A link whose fragment is not a line number is not an anchor and is left alone, however much the file around it moves ([validated by: leaves a link whose fragment is not a line number untouched](../../scripts/repoint-spec-anchors.test.ts#L506)). Test files are the common case, but nothing restricts it to them: a citation into a workflow, a config or a script is repointed the same way ([validated by: repoints docs, README and script citations alike](../../scripts/repoint-spec-anchors.test.ts#L454), [root config](../../scripts/repoint-spec-anchors.test.ts#L476), [workflow and manifest](../../scripts/repoint-spec-anchors.test.ts#L490)).
./target/specs/hal-engine-spec-anchor-drift/spec.md:14:The label is not part of the anchor. A descriptive label such as `[validated by]` is never touched ([validated by: leaves a descriptive label alone while syncing a short-form one beside it](../../scripts/repoint-spec-anchors.test.ts#L420)). A short-form `[Lnn](../../src/X.test.ts#Lnn)` is different: its label carries no meaning beyond the number, so it is force-synced to whatever its own href says ([validated by: syncs labels even in a spec skipped for a differing anchor set](../../scripts/repoint-spec-anchors.test.ts#L399)). Syncing happens after the rewrite, so a repointed href carries its label with it in the same run ([validated by: carries the label along when the href is repointed](../../scripts/repoint-spec-anchors.test.ts#L354)).
./target/specs/hal-engine-spec-anchor-drift/spec.md:20:- That is what makes a second run against the same baseline a no-op rather than a second translation ([validated by: is a no-op the second time, because the baseline is read from the base ref](../../scripts/repoint-spec-anchors.test.ts#L76)).

